            <ArticleTitle>A case of inherited erythromelalgia.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>229-34</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">A 15-year-old boy presented with recurrent episodes of erythema and burning pain in the distal extremities, which he had experienced since early childhood. The episodes were triggered by heat or exertion. His medical history revealed an extensive six-generation family history of similar symptoms.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INVESTIGATIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Neurological examination, MRI brain scan, electromyography, skin biopsy, laboratory blood testing, and DNA analysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DIAGNOSIS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Juvenile onset primary erythromelalgia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MANAGEMENT" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Genetic counseling, and symptomatic management of neuropathic pain.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
